    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "One can observe that the upper bounds on the number of inversions and descents in Proposition 6.1 are tight."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 45,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 235.992, 550.803, 329.512 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Example 24. Consider any positive integers i0,n,k such that i0 < n and k ≥ 2. Let w be a permutation in Sn defined by wi = n+1−i for i ∈ [i0−1] and wi = i−i0+1 for i ∈ {i0,i0+1,...,n}. This is a special case of a valley permutation, where the increasing part consists of smaller numbers than the decreasing part. We obtain that the Lehmer code of this permutation is (cw)j = n − i for each i ∈ [i0 − 1] and (cw)i = 0 for i ∈ {i + 1,i + 2,...,n}. Therefore we obtain from Theorem 4.1 that the number of inversions in the stable configuration Ck,n,w resulting from firing strategy Fw is"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 47,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 99.955, 217.748, 550.506, 235.855 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 10.909,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "i0−1 i=1 ki−1 kn2−i . This is exactly the upper bound on the number of inversions in Ck,n,w for w"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 46,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 77.0, 217.748, 81.429, 234.322 ],
